 undefined, or \\(-1\\)) or forces a third non\u2011sunny line.  Consequently each candidate fails.  \n\n   The slope table for all unordered pairs of points in \\(\\mathcal P\\) is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{Pair }(P,Q) & \\text{slope } \\sigma(P,Q)\\\\\\hline\n   (1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n   (1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n   (1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n   \\text{all other pairs} & 0,\\ \\text{undefined, or }-1\\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a sunny line can contain at most the two points indicated in each of the three \u201csunny pairs\u2019\u2019 above.\n\n   * **\\(x=1\\):** covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); the three uncovered points \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) have only non\u2011sunny pairwise slopes, so two sunny lines can cover at most two of them \u2013 one point stays uncovered.  \n\n   * **\\(x=2\\):** covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\); the uncovered set \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\) contains a single sunny pair \\((1,2),(3,1)\\). After using that line, the remaining points \\((1,1),(1,3)\\) lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\), which would be a second non\u2011sunny line \u2013 impossible.  \n\n   * **\\(x=3\\):** covers \\((3,1)\\); the uncovered five points contain exactly the two sunny pairs \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) and \\((1,3),(2,1)\\). Two sunny lines can cover at most four of the five points, leaving one uncovered.  \n\n   * **\\(y=1\\):** analogous to \\(x=1\\); the three uncovered points have only non\u2011sunny pairwise slopes.  \n\n   * **\\(y=2\\):** covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\); the uncovered set \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\) contains only the sunny pair \\((1,3),(2,1)\\). After using it, the remaining points \\((1,1),(3,1)\\) lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(y=1\\).  \n\n   * **\\(y=3\\):** covers only \\((1,3)\\); the five uncovered points contain the two sunny pairs \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) and \\((1,2),(3,1)\\). As before, two sunny lines can cover at most four points, leaving one uncovered.  \n\n   * **\\(x+y=2\\):** covers only \\((1,1)\\); the five uncovered points admit at most two sunny pairs, so two sunny lines can cover at most four points \u2013 one remains uncovered.  \n\n   * **\\(x+y=3\\):** covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\); the uncovered four points contain only the sunny pair \\((1,1),(2,2)\\). The remaining two points \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=4\\).  \n\n   * **\\(x+y=4\\):** covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\); the three uncovered points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) have only non\u2011sunny pairwise slopes, so two sunny lines can cover at most two of them \u2013 one stays uncovered.  \n\n   In every case the requirement \u201cexactly two sunny lines\u201d cannot be satisfied.  \n\n6. **Conclusion.** Both possibilities for the non\u2011sunny line (meeting or not meeting \\(\\mathcal P\\)) lead to contradictions. Therefore no set of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can cover all points required for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No configuration exists for }k=2\\text{ when }n=3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le4\\) (the condition for \\(n=3\\)).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), undefined (vertical), and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Sunny\u2011line capacity).  \nAny line that contains three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is either \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\), or \\(x+y=4\\); all three are non\u2011sunny. Consequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nCheck all triples of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). The only collinear triples are  \n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\}= \\{x=1\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\}= \\{y=1\\},\n\\]  \n\\[\n\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}= \\{x+y=4\\}.\n\\]  \nEach has slope \\( \\in\\{\\text{undefined},0,-1\\}\\), i.e. non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Non\u2011sunny line must intersect \\(\\mathcal P\\)).  \nIf the unique non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) does **not** contain any point of \\(\\mathcal P\\), then the two sunny lines together can cover at most four points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) (by Lemma\u202f1), leaving at least two points uncovered. Hence such a configuration is impossible.\n\n*Proof.* Directly from Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\nThus the non\u2011sunny line must pass through at least one point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3 (All possible non\u2011sunny lines intersecting \\(\\mathcal P\\)).  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it has an equation of one of the forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\) chosen so that the line meets \\(\\mathcal P\\).  Since the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points in \\(\\mathcal P\\) are \\(1,2,3\\), the possible constants are  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\text{ for }x=c\\text{ or }y=c,\\qquad\nc\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\text{ for }x+y=c.\n\\]  \n\nHence the nine candidate non\u2011sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3,\\\\\n&y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3,\\\\\n&x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 4 (Slope table).  \nFor any unordered pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subset\\mathcal P\\) the slope \\(\\sigma(P,Q)\\) is given by\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\{P,Q\\} & \\sigma(P,Q)\\\\\\hline\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n\\text{all other pairs} & 0,\\ \\text{undefined, or }-1\\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the only sunny lines that can be formed from points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\ (1,1)\\text{\u2013}(2,2),\\qquad\nL_{2}:\\ (1,2)\\text{\u2013}(3,1),\\qquad\nL_{3}:\\ (1,3)\\text{\u2013}(2,1).\n\\]\n\nEach contains exactly the two points listed.\n\n*Proof.* Straightforward computation of slopes for the fifteen unordered pairs of \\(\\mathcal P\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Case analysis.\n\nFor each candidate non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) from Lemma\u202f3 we list \\(\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P\\) and examine the uncovered points \\(\\mathcal P\\setminus\\ell\\).  By Lemma\u202f4 any sunny line can cover at most two of those uncovered points, and the only possible sunny pairs are the three lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).  The exhaustive check is summarized below; in each case we obtain a contradiction.\n\n| Non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) | \\(\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P\\) | Uncovered points | Reason of impossibility |\n|--------------------------|------------------------|------------------|------------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | No sunny pair among them (all slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)). Two sunny lines cover at most two of the three. |\n| \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | Only sunny pair is \\((1,2),(3,1)=L_{2}\\). After using \\(L_{2}\\) the remaining \\((1,1),(1,3)\\) lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\). A second non\u2011sunny line would be required. |\n| \\(x=3\\) | \\((3,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2)\\) | Sunny pairs are \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\); they cover four points, leaving \\((1,2)\\) uncovered. |\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) | No sunny pair among them. |\n| \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | Only sunny pair is \\((1,3),(2,1)=L_{3}\\). Remaining \\((1,1),(3,1)\\) lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(y=1\\). |\n| \\(y=3\\) | \\((1,3)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | Sunny pairs are \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\); they cover four points, leaving one uncovered. |\n| \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | At most two sunny pairs exist, so two sunny lines can cover at most four of the five points. |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | Only sunny pair is \\(L_{1}\\); the remaining \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=4\\). |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) | No sunny pair among them. |\n\nIn every possible placement of the non\u2011sunny line, the two remaining lines cannot be both sunny while covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n\nCombined with Lemma\u202f2, this exhausts all possibilities.\n\n---\n\n#### Final conclusion.\n\nNo set of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition for \\(n=3\\). Hence the required integer \\(k\\) does **not** include \\(k=2\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No configuration exists for }k=2\\text{ when }n=3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have carefully reviewed the \u201cSummary\u2019\u2019 and \u201cDetailed Solution\u2019\u2019 sections to ensure:\n\n* All statements are rigorously justified.\n* No gaps or unproved assertions remain (Lemma\u202f1\u20134 and the case analysis cover every possibility).\n* The LaTeX formatting is correct and all mathematical expressions are enclosed in proper delimiters.\n* The required sections appear in the exact order and with the exact headings prescribed."
