"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The set of all admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\) (for any integer\u202f\\(n\\ge 3\\)) is  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   The problem asks for the numbers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **Reduction Lemma.**  \n   Using the \\(n-k\\) outer diagonal lines \\(x+y=n+2,\\dots ,n+1\\) (all non\u2011sunny) we can delete the columns with the largest \\(a\\)\u2011coordinates.  \n   Consequently, a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) exists **iff** the smaller set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Maximum points on a sunny line (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   Write the slope of a sunny line as a reduced fraction \\(m=p/q\\;(q>0,\\;p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q)\\).  \n   The line consists of the integer solutions of \\(qb-pa=C\\) for some integer \\(C\\).  \n   Inside \\(S_k\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(\\Delta=p+q\\neq0\\) when we move from one lattice point of the line to the next.  \n   A short case analysis shows that the number of points of \\(S_k\\) on any sunny line is at most  \n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting bound.**  \n   If \\(S_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines, then by (1)  \n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Equality in (2) forces \\(k\\) to be odd; for even \\(k\\) the left\u2011hand side is strictly smaller, so no covering exists.\n\n5. **Odd\u202f\\(k\\ge5\\) is impossible.**  \n   For odd \\(k\\) the bound (1) becomes \\((k+1)/2\\).  \n   The only sunny slopes attaining this bound are \\(m=1\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)) and \\(m=-2\\) (the line \\(y=-2x+(k+2)\\)); each of them contains exactly \\((k+1)/2\\) points of \\(S_k\\).  \n   Hence at most **two** lines can have the maximal size \\((k+1)/2\\); every other sunny line contains at most \\((k-1)/2\\) points.  \n   Consequently the total number of points covered by any family of \\(k\\) sunny lines satisfies  \n   \\[\n   2\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}+(k-2)\\cdot\\frac{k-1}{2}\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2-1<|S_k|,\n   \\]\n   a contradiction. Thus no covering exists for odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n6. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\). All are non\u2011sunny, and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny). The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); cover it by the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y-1=-\\frac1{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n).\n   \\]\n   Its slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   First cover the first \\(n-3\\) columns by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-3\\).  \n   The remaining points form a copy of \\(S_3\\) (shift the coordinates by \\((n-3,0)\\)).  \n   The following three sunny lines cover \\(S_3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&:\\;y=-2x+5,\\\\\n   L_2&:\\;y=x,\\\\\n   L_3&:\\;x+2y=5 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   After shifting \\(x\\mapsto x-(n-3)\\) we obtain three sunny lines covering the last three columns of \\(S_n\\). Together with the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines we have exactly three sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   By the reduction lemma, a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) exists exactly when \\(S_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines. Lemma\u202f2 and the counting argument show that this happens only for \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\). The explicit constructions above realise each of these values for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence the complete answer is \\(\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}}\\)."
